STREAM‑PNN: Streaming Tensor‑Reconfigurable Electro‑Optic Architectures for Sub‑Nanosecond Edge AI – Theoretical Foundations and Architecture

Ouadi Maakoul

AI: GEMINI, CLAUDE, Deepseek

---

Abstract

The energy consumption of deep neural networks has become the dominant barrier to deploying intelligent systems at the edge, where milliwatt power budgets preclude conventional digital accelerators. Integrated photonic neural networks promise matrix‑vector multiplication with latency bounded only by the speed of light and energy that scales favourably with throughput, yet they face two fundamental obstacles: the quadratic scaling of active phase shifters in Mach‑Zehnder interferometer meshes and the prohibitive cost of all‑optical nonlinear activation functions. This dissertation develops STREAM‑PNN, a streaming tensor‑reconfigurable electro‑optic architecture that resolves both challenges through a mathematically grounded operator‑splitting framework. Rather than applying an explicit activation after every linear layer, STREAM‑PNN interleaves photonic matrix multiplications with low‑power electro‑optic amplitude modulations; a single optical layer approximates two conventional ReLU layers, halving the number of costly optical‑to‑electronic conversions while preserving expressivity. The framework is supported by rigorous results: an end‑to‑end signal‑power budget with amplified spontaneous emission noise and a closed‑form maximum‑depth condition; hardware‑aware structured sparsity that reduces active MZI count to O(N\log N) using butterfly and low‑rank‑plus‑diagonal decompositions compatible with triangular meshes; fault‑tolerant three‑splitter MZIs with hybrid volatile/phase‑change non‑volatile weight storage and an explicit endurance analysis; a corrected, quantitatively tight operator‑splitting approximation theorem with guaranteed convergence rate; balanced coherent detection that recovers signed pre‑activation values, coupled with a local‑oscillator power budget for wavelength‑division multiplexed operation; an in‑situ adjoint‑variable gradient method with a comprehensive noise model; an adaptive quantization‑and‑noise‑injection training scheme for fabrication resilience; a decomposed latency model demonstrating realistic 2–5 ns inference per sample; a competitive analysis against electronic edge accelerators; and a detailed technology roadmap from sub‑nanojoule to low‑picojoule inference, grounded in specific foundry process nodes. Collectively, these contributions form a rigorous co‑design framework for scalable, trainable, and robust optical neural accelerators, establishing a viable path toward high‑speed, ultra‑low‑energy edge AI.

