1. Introduction

The exponential growth of deep neural networks over the past decade has been fuelled by an equally exponential increase in the energy consumed by the hardware that executes them. Modern large‑scale models require hundreds of picojoules per multiply‑accumulate operation on digital CMOS platforms, and when deployed at the edge—on battery‑powered sensors, wearable devices, or autonomous systems—the energy budget is often capped at a few milliwatts. The root cause of this inefficiency is the von Neumann bottleneck: the physical separation of memory and computation forces data movement to dominate the energy cost of every arithmetic operation. Dennard scaling, which once ensured that transistor energy density improved with each process generation, has stalled, leaving architects with the task of fundamentally rethinking the physical substrate of computation.

Integrated photonics offers a radical alternative. In a coherent optical circuit, the linear transformation \mathbf{y} = \mathbf{W}\mathbf{x} that forms the backbone of every neural network layer can be performed passively through interference. The input vector \mathbf{x} is encoded onto the complex amplitudes of N optical modes, which propagate through a mesh of tunable Mach‑Zehnder interferometers. The resulting field amplitudes at the output ports directly compute the matrix‑vector product, with the latency of the entire operation set by the propagation time of light—typically picoseconds per stage—and the energy cost determined only by the static power to maintain the optical field and program the phase shifters. Wavelength‑division multiplexing further multiplies throughput by carrying independent signals on distinct wavelengths through the same physical mesh without additional active components.

Despite compelling laboratory demonstrations, two obstacles have prevented photonic neural networks from transitioning into deployable hardware. The first is the scaling of the MZI mesh itself. A full unitary mesh requires O(N^2) active phase shifters; for N beyond 32 or 64, thermal crosstalk, calibration complexity, and static power dissipation become prohibitive. The second obstacle is the nonlinear activation function that endows deep networks with expressive power. In the optical domain, all‑optical nonlinearities demand intensities on the order of megawatts per square centimetre, making them utterly unsuitable for low‑power edge devices. The naive alternative—converting the optical signal to the electronic domain after every layer, applying a conventional ReLU, and reconverting back to optics—incurs repeated optical‑to‑electronic conversion losses and, critically, adds latency and power consumption that erode the advantages of photonic computation.

This dissertation proposes STREAM‑PNN—Streaming Tensor‑Reconfigurable Electro‑Optic Architectures—a mathematically grounded co‑design framework that resolves both obstacles simultaneously through a novel operator‑splitting activation scheme and hardware‑aware design principles. The central insight is that the action of two consecutive ReLU layers can be approximated by a single composite linear transformation followed by a carefully designed amplitude modulation mask, provided that the mask parameters are chosen appropriately. In a STREAM‑PNN layer, the MZI mesh first implements the product of two weight matrices \mathbf{W}^{(2k-1)}\mathbf{W}^{(2k)}, absorbing the linear part of both layers. The electro‑optic activation tier then applies a pointwise sigmoidal gate to each output channel, whose cumulative effect across K optical layers exactly approximates the function of a 2K-layer conventional ReLU network. This halves the number of optical‑to‑electronic conversions, dramatically reducing energy and latency, while retaining the full expressivity of the original deep network.

We establish a suite of rigorous theoretical results that provide quantitative guarantees for every aspect of the architecture:

· Phase‑Angle Equivalence (Theorem 2.1): a bijection between phase shifter settings and matrix entries, enabling gradient‑based training directly on the physical chip.
· Closed‑Form Signal‑Power and ASE‑Noise Budget (Theorem 2.2): a recursive model of optical power and amplified spontaneous emission noise in cascaded meshes, yielding an explicit maximum‑depth condition for amplifier placement.
· Structured Sparsity for O(N\log N) MZI Count (Theorem 2.3): a butterfly and low‑rank‑plus‑diagonal decomposition that reduces active MZI count without requiring restrictive spectral gaps, compatible with triangular mesh topology.
· Fault‑Tolerant MZI Design with Hybrid Weight Storage (Definition 2.4): adoption of three‑splitter MZIs and hybrid volatile/phase‑change non‑volatile memory, with an endurance analysis proving amortised write cycles eliminate the endurance bottleneck.
· Tight Operator‑Splitting Approximation (Theorem 3.1): a corrected bound using exponential sigmoid convergence that guarantees any 2K-layer ReLU network can be approximated by a K-layer STREAM‑PNN to arbitrary accuracy with explicit gain requirements.
· Balanced Coherent Detection for Signed Pre‑Activation (Section 3.1, Theorem 5.1.2): recovery of the sign of pre‑activation values using avalanche photodiodes, with a local‑oscillator power budget that confirms shot‑noise‑limited operation.
