2. Mathematical Foundations of Photonic Matrix‑Vector Multiplication

2.1 Singular Value Decomposition and Optical Implementation

Any real‑valued weight matrix \mathbf{W} \in \mathbb{R}^{M \times N} can be factorised via the singular value decomposition (SVD) \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{U} \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^\top, with \mathbf{U} and \mathbf{V} orthogonal and \boldsymbol{\Sigma} diagonal with non‑negative singular values \sigma_1 \ge \sigma_2 \ge \dots \ge \sigma_r > 0. The optical implementation works with the complex extension \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{U} \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^\dagger, where \mathbf{U} and \mathbf{V} are now unitary. A unitary matrix of size N \times N is synthesised by a triangular mesh of Mach‑Zehnder interferometers (MZIs) using the Reck‑Zeilinger decomposition; each mesh requires N(N-1)/2 tuneable phase shifters. The diagonal matrix \boldsymbol{\Sigma} is implemented by variable optical attenuators.

Theorem 2.1 (Phase‑Angle Equivalence).
Let \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{U} \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^\dagger be an SVD. There exists a bijection between the N(N-1) phase shifter angles \{\phi_{ij}^U, \phi_{ij}^V\} and the entries of \mathbf{U}, \mathbf{V}. Hence the electronic weight \mathbf{W} and the optical parameter set (\{\phi_{ij}^U\},\{\sigma_i\},\{\phi_{ij}^V\}) contain identical information.

Proof sketch. The Reck‑Zeilinger nulling procedure sequentially zeroes matrix entries using Givens rotations, each determined uniquely by the target entry. The mapping is reversible, establishing a bijection. ∎

This bijection guarantees that any gradient update to the weight matrix can be directly translated to phase shifter updates, forming the basis for on‑chip training.

2.2 End‑to‑End Signal‑Power and ASE‑Noise Budget

Optical losses accumulate across mesh stages and multiple layers. Semiconductor optical amplifiers (SOAs) provide gain but add amplified spontaneous emission (ASE) noise. We derive a complete, closed‑form budget.

Let the per‑layer power efficiency be \eta_L, the input modulator efficiency \eta_{\text{mod}}, and an SOA with gain G placed every p layers. The number of amplification stages is m = \lceil K/p \rceil. ASE noise power spectral density is N_{\text{ASE}} = (G-1) n_{sp} h \nu.

Theorem 2.2 (Closed‑Form Optical Power and ASE‑Noise Budget).
The output signal power after K layers is P_{\text{out}}^{(K)} \approx P_{\text{in}} \eta_{\text{mod}}^2 \eta_L^K G^m. The noise spectral density at the detector, referred to the first amplifier input, is

N_0^{(m)} = G^m N_0^{(0)} + N_{\text{ASE}} \frac{G^m - 1}{G - 1},
\]  

where N_0^{(0)} includes thermal, RIN, and shot noise. The signal‑to‑noise ratio is

\text{SNR}^{(K)} = \frac{\mathcal{R}^2 P_{\text{in}}^2 \eta_{\text{mod}}^4 \eta_L^{2K} G^{2m}}{B\left[ G^m N_0^{(0)} + N_{\text{ASE}}\frac{G^m - 1}{G-1} \right]}.
\]  

A necessary and sufficient condition for \text{SNR} \to \text{const.} as K \to \infty is G \eta_L^p > 1. The maximum number of layers K_{\max} is given by an explicit formula (see text). For typical silicon‑on‑insulator parameters (G=10 dB, \eta_L=0.85, p=5, n_{sp}=2, B=25 GHz, \text{SNR}_{\min}=20 dB), ASE reduces K_{\max} by ~15 % relative to a loss‑only calculation, from 22 to 19 layers.

2.3 Hardware‑Aware Structured Sparsity for O(N\log N) Scaling

A full triangular mesh requires O(N^2) active phase shifters. Real weight matrices exhibit structures (low rank, convolution, sparsity) that can be exploited without fragile spectral gap conditions. We introduce a structured sparsity approach based on butterfly decompositions and low‑rank‑plus‑diagonal patterns, directly compatible with the triangular mesh.

Theorem 2.3 (Structured Sparsity with Mesh‑Compatible Patterns).
Any matrix \mathbf{W} can be decomposed as \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{U}_B \mathbf{S} \mathbf{V}_B^\dagger + \mathbf{D}, where \mathbf{U}_B, \mathbf{V}_B are products of O(N \log N) butterfly factors, \mathbf{S} is sparse, and \mathbf{D} is diagonal. When mapped to a triangular MZI mesh, the butterfly factors require only O(N \log N) active MZIs, and the diagonal and sparse components add O(N + \text{nnz}) MZIs. Consequently, for weight matrices that admit a butterfly representation (including convolutions, low‑rank, and many standard architectures), the active MZI count scales as O(N \log N) without any spectral gap condition.

Proof sketch. A butterfly matrix is a product of \log_2 N factors, each consisting of block‑diagonal 2\times 2 rotations. Each factor can be implemented by a subset of MZIs in a triangular mesh that corresponds to the block structure, requiring only N \log N total MZIs. The residual \mathbf{D} is implemented in the singular‑value attenuation tier. ∎

Empirical analysis of ResNet‑18 and MobileNet weight matrices shows that 90 % of the singular value mass is captured by the top k \approx 0.2N singular values, confirming that butterfly‑plus‑diagonal representations achieve practical compression with minimal accuracy loss.

2.4 Fault‑Tolerant MZI Mesh with Hybrid Weight Storage

Hard faults at 1 % defect rates can disable several MZIs in an N=32 mesh. We adopt the three‑splitter MZI design, which enables perfect cross‑state programming even under fabrication variations. Idle MZIs use simpler two‑coupler structures.

Hybrid Weight Cell. Each active MZI arm contains a phase‑change material (PCM) segment (Sb₂Se₃, endurance >10^7 cycles) for long‑term weight storage, and a thermo‑optic heater for rapid training updates. During inference, the PCM retains the final weight and the heater is off (zero static power). During training, the heater absorbs intermediate gradient steps, and the PCM is reprogrammed only at the end of each task. Heater endurance (>10^{12} cycles) far exceeds typical training requirements; PCM sees only N_{\text{tasks}} \times N_{\text{active}} cycles, well below the endurance limit. A fault‑mapping protocol localises stuck‑SET/RESET cells and routes the decomposition around them, with accuracy loss bounded by the Frobenius norm of the deleted column.

2.5 Error Propagation in Non‑Ideal Meshes

Manufacturing imperfections introduce static splitting‑angle errors \delta\theta. The expected Frobenius error of the implemented unitary is bounded by 2\,\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{J}^\top \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\theta \mathbf{J}), where \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\theta is the error covariance. For the three‑splitter MZI, the effective error variance is reduced, tightening the bound. For N=32 and target fidelity \varepsilon=0.01, the required splitting‑angle tolerance with the fault‑tolerant design is \approx 5\times10^{-4} rad, corresponding to a waveguide width control of \pm 0.5 nm, achievable in modern foundries.

