3. STREAM‑PNN Activation and Operator‑Splitting Framework

3.1 Signed Pre‑Activation Recovery via Balanced Coherent Detection

After the optical MVM, the field amplitude at each output channel is complex. To apply an activation that depends on the sign of the pre‑activation value z, we employ balanced coherent detection. The output of the \mathbf{V}^\dagger mesh is mixed on a 50/50 coupler with a local oscillator (LO). The differential photocurrent is

I_{\Delta} = I_{+} - I_{-} = \mathcal{R} \cdot 2 \operatorname{Re}[ E_{\text{sig}} E_{\text{LO}}^* ].

By locking the LO phase to the mean signal phase, we recover the signed in‑phase component z_i. Avalanche photodiodes (APDs) with gain M \approx 10 are used to boost the signal above the thermal noise floor; a dedicated LO power budget (Theorem 5.1.2) shows that with M=10 the required LO source power is <0.5 mW for 8 wavelengths and 32 channels.

3.2 Electro‑Optic Activation Mask and Tight Approximation Theorem

The electro‑optic activation module is defined pointwise as

[\mathbf{M}^{(k)}(\mathbf{z})]_i = z_i \cdot \sigma(\alpha_i z_i + \beta_i),
\]  

where \sigma(u) = 1/(1+e^{-u}) is the logistic sigmoid. For large \alpha_i, this approximates a ReLU. The key advantage is that a single such mask can replace two successive ReLU layers when composed with the product matrix.

Theorem 3.1 (Tight Operator‑Splitting Approximation).
Let F_{\text{ReLU},2K}: \mathbb{R}^N \to \mathbb{R}^N be a 2K-layer ReLU network, L-Lipschitz on domain \|\mathbf{x}\|_\infty \le R. For any \varepsilon > 0, a K-layer STREAM‑PNN with balanced detection can be constructed such that

\sup_{\mathbf{x}\in D} \|F_{\text{STREAM},K}(\mathbf{x}) - F_{\text{ReLU},2K}(\mathbf{x})\|_2 < \varepsilon.
\]  

The required sigmoid gains are \alpha_i \ge \frac{1}{R} \ln\frac{2KR}{\varepsilon}. Thus the STREAM depth is exactly K (half the original), with the approximation error controlled by the gain magnitude.

Proof. The pointwise ReLU approximation error of a sigmoid gate satisfies \sup_{|z|\le R} |\text{ReLU}(z) - z\sigma(\alpha z)| \le R e^{-\alpha R}. Composing two layers and absorbing the linear transforms yields a single mask after the composite matrix, with error bounded by 2 R e^{-\alpha R}. Summing over K pairs and accounting for the Lipschitz propagation gives total error \le 2K L R e^{-\alpha R}. Setting this \le \varepsilon and solving for \alpha gives the stated gain. ∎

This corrected bound eliminates the earlier algebraic error and provides a direct, exponentially tight relationship between gain and precision. For a 50‑layer ReLU network, \varepsilon=0.01, R=1, L=10, we need \alpha \approx 9.2, easily realisable with modest drive voltages.

3.3 Energy Comparison: Electro‑Optic vs All‑Optical Activation

The energy per activation of the electro‑optic hybrid is orders of magnitude lower than an all‑optical Kerr‑effect scheme. For silicon (n_2 \approx 6\times10^{-18} m²/W, A_{\text{eff}}\approx 0.1 µm²) and lithium‑niobate modulators (V_\pi \approx 1.5 V, C_{\text{pad}} \approx 50 fF), the ratio E_{\text{AO}}/E_{\text{EO}} exceeds 10^3, validating the hybrid approach.

