6. Complexity and Information‑Theoretic Bounds

6.1 VC Dimension of SVD‑Parameterised Networks

The VC dimension of a K-layer STREAM‑PNN with N modes is bounded between \frac{N(N-1)K}{2} and N^2 K \log_2(NK). When structured sparsity is used, the effective parameter count reduces proportionally.

6.2 Minimum Code Length with Quantization‑Aware Training

The effective bit precision of singular values under phase noise \sigma_\phi and DAC quantization \sigma_Q is b_{\text{eff}} = b - \log_2(1 + \sigma_Q^2/\sigma_\phi^2). For N=32, b=8, \sigma_\phi=2 mrad, \sigma_Q=0.5 mrad (10‑bit DAC), b_{\text{eff}} \approx 7.91 bits, confirming that 10‑bit DACs suffice for 8‑bit weights.

6.3 The Analog Precision Wall

Analog computing, including photonics, faces a fundamental signal‑to‑noise limit. In a MZI mesh, the effective number of bits (ENOB) is bounded by the accumulated phase error and detection noise. We analyze the trade‑off between depth and precision: each additional optical layer adds shot noise and phase drift, effectively reducing ENOB by ~0.2 bits per layer. For K=5 layers, 8‑bit precision is viable; extending beyond 10 layers may require a mixed‑precision scheme where early layers use 8‑bit and later layers 4‑bit, following a layer‑wise sensitivity analysis. Recent PACE measurements (7.61 ENOB at 64×64) and adaptive training results (AQ‑PANN) demonstrate that 6–8 bits are achievable with proper co‑design, and our framework provides the theoretical bounds to guide such designs.

