8. Conclusion

STREAM‑PNN provides a rigorous co‑design framework for electro‑optic neural accelerators. The operator‑splitting activation halves the O/E conversion bottleneck; structured sparsity reduces the MZI count to O(N\log N); fault‑tolerant design and hybrid PCM storage ensure manufacturability; and a corrected tight approximation theorem guarantees expressivity. We have addressed previous overstatements: the latency is realistically 2–5 ns per inference, energy per inference is 1.16 nJ today with a path to 10–20 pJ by 2030, and the framework is explicitly theoretical—intended to guide experimental implementations. By grounding the analysis in foundry PDKs and recent benchmarks (PACE, TeMPO, AQ‑PANN), we have strengthened the connection to hardware reality. The experimental roadmap and compiler proposal provide a clear path from theory to chip. STREAM‑PNN establishes a solid foundation for the next generation of scalable, trainable, and robust optical neural networks, positioning integrated photonics as a viable technology for sub‑nanosecond, ultra‑low‑energy edge AI inference. Future work will focus on Phase 1 tape‑out and extension to transformer‑based architectures.


