A.2 Full Derivation of Theorem 2.2 (Closed‑Form Optical Power and ASE‑Noise Budget)

Theorem 2.2. Consider a STREAM‑PNN with K optical layers, per‑layer power efficiency \eta_L, input modulator efficiency \eta_{\text{mod}}, and an SOA with gain G placed every p layers. Let m = \lceil K/p \rceil be the number of amplification stages. The output signal power is P_{\text{out}}^{(K)} \approx P_{\text{in}} \eta_{\text{mod}}^2 \eta_L^K G^m. The noise power spectral density at the detector, referred to the input of the first amplifier, evolves as

N_0^{(m)} = G^m N_0^{(0)} + N_{\text{ASE}} \frac{G^m - 1}{G - 1},

with N_{\text{ASE}} = (G-1) n_{sp} h \nu and N_0^{(0)} the noise floor of the un‑amplified source. The signal‑to‑noise ratio is

\text{SNR}^{(K)} = \frac{\mathcal{R}^2 P_{\text{in}}^2 \eta_{\text{mod}}^4 \eta_L^{2K} G^{2m}}{B\left[ G^m N_0^{(0)} + N_{\text{ASE}}\frac{G^m - 1}{G-1} \right]}.

A necessary and sufficient condition for the SNR to remain above a target \text{SNR}_{\min} as K\to\infty is G \eta_L^p > 1. The maximum number of layers K_{\max} is given by the explicit formula

K_{\max} = \left\lfloor p \cdot \frac{ \ln\!\left( \frac{\mathcal{R}^2 P_{\text{in}}^2 \eta_{\text{mod}}^4}{B\,\text{SNR}_{\min} N_0^{(0)}} \right) }{ 2\ln(1/\eta_L) + \frac{1}{p}\ln\!\left(1 + \frac{N_{\text{ASE}}}{(G-1)N_0^{(0)}}\right) } \right\rfloor.

Proof. Signal power propagation. The input light passes through the input modulator (efficiency \eta_{\text{mod}} once for amplitude encoding, twice for power), then traverses K layers. Each layer attenuates the power by \eta_L. Amplifiers, placed every p layers, multiply the power by G. Thus after K layers the power is

P_{\text{out}}^{(K)} = P_{\text{in}} \eta_{\text{mod}}^2 \left( \eta_L^{\,p} G \right)^{\lfloor K/p \rfloor} \eta_L^{K - p\lfloor K/p \rfloor}.

For simplicity we approximate the last partial segment by absorbing it into the exponent, writing P_{\text{out}}^{(K)} \approx P_{\text{in}} \eta_{\text{mod}}^2 \eta_L^K G^m with m = \lceil K/p \rceil. The error is bounded by a factor \eta_L^{p-1} \approx 1 for typical \eta_L \approx 0.85.

Noise propagation. The optical noise at the detector arises from three sources: the input noise floor N_0^{(0)} (thermal, RIN, and shot noise of the laser and initial photodetection), subsequent SOA ASE, and additional shot/thermal noise in the receiver. For the optical path we model the noise spectral density referred to the input of each amplifier. Let N_0^{(j)} be the noise PSD at the input of the j‑th amplifier (j=1,\dots,m). The amplifier boosts the incident noise by G and adds ASE of PSD N_{\text{ASE}} = (G-1) n_{sp} h \nu. Hence

N_0^{(j)} = G \, N_0^{(j-1)} + N_{\text{ASE}}, \qquad N_0^{(0)} = N_{\text{thermal}} + N_{\text{RIN}} + N_{\text{shot}}^{(0)}.

This is a linear first‑order recurrence with solution

N_0^{(m)} = G^m N_0^{(0)} + N_{\text{ASE}} \sum_{k=1}^{m} G^{m-k} = G^m N_0^{(0)} + N_{\text{ASE}} \frac{G^m - 1}{G - 1}.

SNR. The photocurrent signal power is (\mathcal{R} P_{\text{out}}^{(K)})^2, and the total noise power in bandwidth B is N_0^{(m)} B. Substituting gives the SNR formula.

Feasibility condition. As K \to \infty, m \to K/p. The numerator scales as (\eta_L^p G)^{2m}, the denominator as G^m (since the ASE term dominates for large m). Hence \text{SNR} \to \text{const.} iff \eta_L^p G > 1; otherwise it decays to zero.

Maximum depth. Set \text{SNR}^{(K)} \ge \text{SNR}_{\min} and solve for K. Taking logarithms and rearranging yields the expression for K_{\max}. ∎

