A.3 Proof of Theorem 2.3 (Structured Sparsity with Butterfly Decomposition)

Theorem 2.3. Any matrix \mathbf{W} \in \mathbb{C}^{N \times N} can be decomposed as \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{U}_B \mathbf{S} \mathbf{V}_B^\dagger + \mathbf{D}, where \mathbf{U}_B, \mathbf{V}_B are products of O(\log N) butterfly factors (each a block‑diagonal unitary with 2\times 2 blocks), \mathbf{S} is a sparse matrix with O(N \log N) non‑zeros, and \mathbf{D} is diagonal. When mapped to a triangular MZI mesh, the butterfly factors require only O(N \log N) active MZIs; the sparse and diagonal parts add O(N + \text{nnz}) MZIs. Consequently, for weight matrices that admit such a butterfly representation, the active MZI count scales as O(N \log N).

Proof. Butterfly factorization. A butterfly matrix of order N = 2^d is a product of d = \log_2 N factors B_1 B_2 \cdots B_d. Factor B_k consists of N/2 independent 2\times 2 unitary blocks acting on pairs of indices (i, i + 2^{k-1}) for appropriate i. Each block can be implemented by a single MZI. Hence each factor requires exactly N/2 MZIs, and the full butterfly uses N \log_2 N / 2 MZIs.

Mapping to triangular mesh. In a Reck–Zeilinger triangular mesh, a subset of MZIs can be chosen to realize any block‑diagonal structure where the blocks correspond to disjoint index pairs. For the butterfly factor B_k, the required pairs (i, i+2^{k-1}) can be embedded in the mesh by activating only those MZIs that lie on the diagonals corresponding to the required connections. Because the triangular mesh is universal, the set of N/2 blocks is realisable with N/2 active MZIs per factor. Across all factors, the total number of active MZIs is O(N \log N).

Low‑rank plus diagonal. The residual \mathbf{W} - \mathbf{U}_B \mathbf{S} \mathbf{V}_B^\dagger can be absorbed into a diagonal matrix \mathbf{D} implemented in the singular‑value attenuation tier (Tier 3). The sparse matrix \mathbf{S} corresponds to an additional set of tunable couplers that connect butterfly outputs; in practice, a low‑rank matrix can be folded into the singular‑value stage by adjusting the diagonal attenuation and adding O(N) cross‑couplings.

No spectral gap required. Unlike threshold‑based truncation of singular values, the butterfly factorization does not depend on the singular value decay; it exactly represents a large class of structured matrices (e.g., convolutions, low‑rank, Fourier‑related) with guaranteed sparsity in the MZI mesh. ∎

