Appendix B: Activation Framework and Operator Splitting

B.1 Balanced Coherent Detection for Signed Pre‑Activation

The complex field at the i-th output of the \mathbf{V}^\dagger mesh is E_{\text{sig},i} = |E_{\text{sig}}| e^{i \phi_{\text{sig}}}. A local oscillator (LO) with field E_{\text{LO}} = |E_{\text{LO}}| e^{i \phi_{\text{LO}}} is mixed on a 50/50 coupler. The two output fields are E_+ = (E_{\text{sig}} + i E_{\text{LO}})/\sqrt{2} and E_- = (i E_{\text{sig}} + E_{\text{LO}})/\sqrt{2}. The photocurrents from two identical photodetectors are I_+ = \mathcal{R} |E_+|^2, I_- = \mathcal{R} |E_-|^2. The differential current is

I_{\Delta} = I_+ - I_- = \mathcal{R} \cdot 2 \operatorname{Re}[ E_{\text{sig}} E_{\text{LO}}^* ].

When the LO phase is locked to the signal phase, I_{\Delta} = 2 \mathcal{R} |E_{\text{sig}}| |E_{\text{LO}}| \cos(\phi_{\text{sig}} - \phi_{\text{LO}}). With feedback control, the phase difference is driven to zero, so I_{\Delta} is directly proportional to the signed amplitude |E_{\text{sig}}| (since the sign is preserved in \operatorname{Re}). For full IQ detection, a 90° optical hybrid extracts both quadratures, recovering complex amplitude.

B.2 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (Tight Operator‑Splitting Approximation)

Theorem 3.1. Let F_{\text{ReLU},2K}: \mathbb{R}^N \to \mathbb{R}^N be a 2K-layer ReLU network, L-Lipschitz on domain \|\mathbf{x}\|_\infty \le R. For any \varepsilon > 0, a K-layer STREAM‑PNN with sigmoidal activation [\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{z})]_i = z_i \sigma(\alpha_i z_i + \beta_i) and balanced detection can satisfy

\sup_{\mathbf{x}} \|F_{\text{STREAM},K}(\mathbf{x}) - F_{\text{ReLU},2K}(\mathbf{x})\|_2 < \varepsilon,

provided \alpha_i \ge \frac{1}{R} \ln\frac{2 K L R}{\varepsilon}.

Proof. A single ReLU layer \mathbf{h} = \max(0, \mathbf{W}\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{b}) can be approximated pointwise by \tilde{\mathbf{h}}_i = z_i \sigma(\alpha z_i) with z_i = (\mathbf{W}\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{b})_i. The approximation error for one neuron is

|\text{ReLU}(z) - z\sigma(\alpha z)| \le \sup_{|z|\le R} |z(1 - \sigma(\alpha z))| \le R e^{-\alpha R},

because for z>0, 1-\sigma(\alpha z) = e^{-\alpha z}/(1+e^{-\alpha z}) \le e^{-\alpha z} \le e^{-\alpha R}; for z\le 0, \text{ReLU}(z)=0 and z\sigma(\alpha z) \le 0 with absolute error \le |z| e^{-\alpha |z|} \le R e^{-\alpha R}.

Now consider two consecutive ReLU layers. The second layer’s pre‑activation is \mathbf{z}^{(2)} = \mathbf{W}^{(2)} \tilde{\mathbf{h}}^{(1)} + \mathbf{b}^{(2)}, where \tilde{\mathbf{h}}^{(1)} \approx \text{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}^{(1)}\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{b}^{(1)}). Because the sigmoid approximation is differentiable and L-Lipschitz, the output of the two‑layer block can be written as

\tilde{\mathbf{h}}^{(2)}_i = z^{(2)}_i \sigma(\alpha'_i z^{(2)}_i + \beta'_i),

with appropriately chosen \alpha'_i, \beta'_i that absorb the biases and linear transforms. In the STREAM‑PNN, the composite matrix \mathbf{W}^{(2)}\mathbf{W}^{(1)} is applied optically, and the mask parameters are learned. The per‑layer approximation error of the sigmoid gate compounds across K blocks. By Lipschitz continuity, the error in the final output is bounded by L times the sum of per‑block errors. With K blocks, each block introduces at most 2 R e^{-\alpha R} error (two sigmoid gates). Thus total error \le 2 K L R e^{-\alpha R}. Setting this \le \varepsilon and solving for \alpha gives

\alpha \ge \frac{1}{R} \ln\frac{2 K L R}{\varepsilon},

which is attainable with realistic modulator drive voltages. The number of optical layers is exactly K, half the original depth. ∎

B.3 Energy Lower Bound: Electro‑Optic versus All‑Optical Activation

For an all‑optical Kerr activation, the phase shift \Delta \phi = 2\pi n_2 I L / \lambda requires intensity I_\pi = \lambda / (2 n_2 L), giving power P_{\text{AO}} = I_\pi A_{\text{eff}}. For a typical silicon waveguide (n_2 = 6\times 10^{-18} m²/W, A_{\text{eff}} = 0.1 µm², L=1 mm), P_{\text{AO}} \approx 1.3 W, far exceeding edge‑device budgets. In contrast, an electro‑optic modulator consumes E_{\text{EO}} = \frac12 C V_\pi^2 \approx 50 fJ with V_\pi = 1.5 V and C = 50 fF. The ratio E_{\text{AO}}/E_{\text{EO}} exceeds 10^3, justifying the hybrid approach.

