Appendix C: Learning Theory and On‑Chip Optimization

C.1 Derivation of Adjoint Variable Method (AVM) with Full Noise Model

The photonic network is described by the linear system \mathbf{A}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) \mathbf{E} = \mathbf{s}, where \mathbf{A} is the system matrix, \mathbf{E} the complex field amplitudes, and \mathbf{s} the input source. The loss L = \ell(\mathbf{E}_{\text{out}}) is a real‑valued function of the output fields.

Gradient expression. Differentiating L with respect to a phase parameter \theta_k:

\frac{\partial L}{\partial \theta_k} = 2 \Re\!\left[ \left( \frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{E}} \right)^\dagger \frac{\partial \mathbf{E}}{\partial \theta_k} \right].

From \mathbf{A} \mathbf{E} = \mathbf{s}, \frac{\partial \mathbf{E}}{\partial \theta_k} = -\mathbf{A}^{-1} \frac{\partial \mathbf{A}}{\partial \theta_k} \mathbf{E}. Substituting and defining the adjoint field \boldsymbol{\lambda} via

\mathbf{A}^\dagger \boldsymbol{\lambda} = \left( \frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{E}} \right)^\dagger,

we obtain

\frac{\partial L}{\partial \theta_k} = -2 \Re\!\left[ \boldsymbol{\lambda}^\dagger \frac{\partial \mathbf{A}}{\partial \theta_k} \mathbf{E} \right].

Since \mathbf{A} for an MZI mesh is composed of block‑diagonal coupling matrices, \partial \mathbf{A}/\partial \theta_k is extremely sparse; the product reduces to a combination of the forward field \mathbf{E} and the backward adjoint field \boldsymbol{\lambda} at the ports of MZI k. This can be measured photoelectrically.

Physical implementation. The adjoint field \boldsymbol{\lambda} is generated by time‑reversing the forward field, injecting the conjugated loss gradient -\partial L/\partial \mathbf{E}^* at the output, and propagating backward through the mesh with conjugated phase shifts. The required gradient for each MZI is proportional to the interference of the forward and adjoint fields at its output coupler, directly measurable by a dedicated pair of photodetectors.

Noise model. The measured photocurrent \hat{g}_k = \eta_{\text{det}} g_k + n_k, where g_k is the ideal gradient component and n_k aggregates noise from:

· Shot noise: \sigma_{\text{shot}}^2 = 2 q I_{\text{dc}} B, with I_{\text{dc}} the DC photocurrent.
· Relative intensity noise (RIN): \sigma_{\text{RIN}}^2 = (\text{RIN}) I_{\text{dc}}^2 B.
· Thermal noise of TIA: \sigma_{\text{thermal}}^2 = 4 k_B T B / R_{\text{TIA}}.
· Two‑photon absorption (TPA): \sigma_{\text{TPA}}^2 \approx \kappa P_{\text{opt}}^2 B.
· ASE beat noise: \sigma_{\text{ASE}}^2 = 2 \mathcal{R}^2 P_{\text{sig}} N_{\text{ASE}} B.

All are independent Gaussian processes. For input powers \le 0.5 mW and APD gain M=10, shot noise dominates, and the gradient SNR is \gtrsim 20 dB.

C.2 Adaptive Quantization and Noise Injection (AQ‑PANN)

To train networks robust to fabrication errors without inverting large covariance matrices, we adopt a scheme where during the forward pass each phase angle is quantized to a learnable step size \Delta, and spatially correlated Gaussian noise with the measured covariance \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\theta is injected at the photonic multiply–accumulate (MAC) level. The backward pass uses the straight‑through estimator (STE). The quantization step sizes are themselves optimised via gradient descent.

The effective bit precision after training is b_{\text{eff}} = b - \log_2(1 + \sigma_Q^2/\sigma_\phi^2), where \sigma_Q^2 = \Delta^2/12 and \sigma_\phi is the phase noise standard deviation. For b=8 and \sigma_\phi = 2 mrad, a 10‑bit DAC (\sigma_Q \approx 0.5 mrad) yields b_{\text{eff}} \approx 7.91 bits.

C.3 Loss Landscape and Curriculum Training

At zero activation gain (\boldsymbol{\alpha}=0), the STREAM‑PNN reduces to a deep linear network parameterized by unitary matrices. It is known (Kawaguchi, 2016) that such networks have no spurious local minima: every local minimum is global and every saddle point has a strictly negative Hessian eigenvalue. Because the phase‑angle mapping is bijective (Theorem 2.1), the same holds for the phase parameterisation.

For small \boldsymbol{\alpha}, the activation is a smooth perturbation of the identity. The loss landscape changes continuously; as long as \|\boldsymbol{\alpha}\| < \alpha^*, where \alpha^* depends on the condition number of the weight matrices, no new local minima appear. The curriculum schedule \alpha_i(t) = \alpha_{\text{final}}(1 - e^{-t/\hat{\tau}}) keeps the nonlinearity within this safe region during training. The time constant \hat{\tau} is estimated on‑chip by power iteration on the Hessian of the deep linear network, requiring only a few additional forward–backward passes.

C.4 Robustness to Weight Drift

PCM storage exhibits retention >10 years at 85 °C. Short‑term thermal fluctuations cause a phase drift of <1 mrad/°C. The fault‑mapping protocol (Section 2.4) can be periodically executed using stored test vectors to recalibrate the mesh. Monte Carlo simulations show that with a recalibration every 10 K temperature change, the accuracy drops by <0.05 %, sufficient for edge deployment.

