Appendix D: Architecture Proofs and Models

D.1 Latency Decomposition

The total latency of a single STREAM‑PNN layer is the sum of:

1. Optical propagation: L_{\text{mesh}} / v_g. For a mesh depth L = N(N-1)/2 MZIs and waveguide length ~100 µm per MZI, propagation time is ~0.5 ns for N=32.
2. Modulator rise time: 0.35 / f_{\text{3dB}}. With 50 GHz modulators, rise time ≈ 7 ps; the full DAC‑driven transition including settling adds ~20 ps.
3. Detection: APD avalanche build‑up time ≈ 10 ps, TIA bandwidth 25 GHz gives ~14 ps rise time; total detection latency ~40 ps.
4. Electronic processing: DAC settling (300 ps), comparator/LUT (200 ps), control logic (200 ps), totaling ~0.7–1 ns.

Pipelining can overlap optical propagation of layer k+1 with electronic processing of layer k, resulting in an effective per‑layer latency of ~0.8 ns. For K=5 layers, end‑to‑end inference latency is ≈ 2–5 ns.

D.2 Local Oscillator Power Budget (Theorem 5.1.2)

With an APD of gain M and excess noise factor F(M) \approx M^{0.3}, the shot noise at the differential TIA output is \sigma_{\text{shot}}^2 = 4 q \eta_{\text{det}} M^2 F(M) P_{\text{LO,ch}} B. The thermal noise is \sigma_{\text{thermal}}^2 = 4 k_B T B / R_{\text{TIA}}. For shot‑noise‑limited detection we require \sigma_{\text{shot}}^2 \ge \sigma_{\text{thermal}}^2, giving

P_{\text{LO,ch}} \ge \frac{k_B T}{q \eta_{\text{det}} M^2 F(M) R_{\text{TIA}}}.

With M=10, F \approx 2, R_{\text{TIA}}=1 kΩ, T=300 K, \eta_{\text{det}}=0.8, we obtain P_{\text{LO,ch}} \ge 1.3 µW. After splitting to W N = 8 \times 32 = 256 channels with 80% splitter efficiency, the source LO power is 0.42 mW, easily met by a 1 mW laser.

D.3 WDM Crosstalk with Regularised AWG Pre‑compensation

The total inter‑channel crosstalk power is C = C_{\text{split}} + C_{\text{Raman}} + C_{\text{AWG}} + C_{\text{XPM}}. The dominant term is often the AWG leakage. We pre‑compensate by digitally multiplying the input vector by \mathbf{A}_{\text{corr}} = (\mathbf{S}_{\text{AWG}}^\dagger \mathbf{S}_{\text{AWG}} + \mu \mathbf{I})^{-1} \mathbf{S}_{\text{AWG}}^\dagger, where \mu = \sigma_{\text{noise}}^2 / \sigma_{\text{signal}}^2. The residual crosstalk after compensation is \|\mathbf{S}_{\text{AWG}}\mathbf{A}_{\text{corr}} - \mathbf{I}\|_F^2 + \mu \|\mathbf{A}_{\text{corr}}\|_F^2. For typical foundry PDK data (IMEC iSiPP50G), an 8‑channel AWG with 3.2 nm spacing and \mu=0.01 achieves −35 dB crosstalk, sufficient for negligible accuracy loss.

D.4 Differentiable Spectral‑Norm Penalty (Theorem 5.5)

For a recurrent weight matrix \mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}}, stability requires \sigma_{\max}(\mathbf{W}_{\text{rec}}) < 4 (since \max \sigma' \le 1/4). We add the penalty \mathcal{P} = \lambda_{\text{stab}} \max(0, \sigma_{\max} - 4)^2. The gradient with respect to \mathbf{W} is

\nabla_{\mathbf{W}} \mathcal{P} = 2 \lambda_{\text{stab}} \max(0, \sigma_{\max} - 4) \, \mathbf{u} \mathbf{v}^\top,

where \mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v} are left and right singular vectors of \sigma_{\max}. The power iteration on \mathbf{W}^\top\mathbf{W} using the AVM infrastructure (one forward and one adjoint pass per iteration) provides estimates of \mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v} and \sigma_{\max}. This differentiable penalty integrates seamlessly into gradient‑based training.

