Appendix F: Additional Architectural Details

F.1 Reck–Zeilinger Decomposition Algorithm

1. Start with target unitary \mathbf{U}.
2. For column j = 1 to N:
   · For row i = N down to j+1:
     · Compute \theta = \arctan(|U_{jj}| / |U_{ij}|), \phi = \arg(U_{jj} / U_{ij}).
     · Form rotation T_{ij}(\theta,\phi) and update \mathbf{U} \leftarrow T_{ij} \mathbf{U}.
3. After all nulling steps, \mathbf{U} = \mathbf{I}. The product of inverse rotations reconstructs the original matrix.

Each rotation is implemented by an MZI with phase shifters set to \theta and \phi.

F.2 Three‑Splitter MZI Transfer Matrix

The three‑splitter MZI has three couplers with nominal splitting ratios 50:50, 50:50, and 100:0 (or a third tunable coupler). The overall transfer matrix can be written as

T_{3\text{-split}} = e^{i\Phi} \begin{pmatrix} e^{i\phi_1} \sin\theta & \cos\theta \\ \cos\theta & -e^{-i\phi_1} \sin\theta \end{pmatrix},

where the extra degree of freedom \phi_1 enables perfect cross‑state programming even with imperfect couplers.

F.3 Butterfly Factorization and Mesh Mapping

A butterfly factor B_k with 2\times 2 blocks on indices (i, i+2^{k-1}) is mapped to the triangular mesh by activating MZIs that lie on the path connecting those indices. In the Reck–Zeilinger mesh, each row i and column j MZI can be assigned to a specific pair interaction. By activating only a subset, the full butterfly product is realised with O(N \log N) active MZIs.

F.4 Hybrid PCM/Heater Endurance Calculation

· Heater cycles: Training for 10 hours with 1 kHz update frequency → 3.6 \times 10^7 cycles, well within >10^{12} endurance.
· PCM cycles: Only reprogrammed at the end of each task. For 10 tasks, 10 \times N_{\text{active}} \approx 5,000 cycles, far below the 10^7 endurance limit.
· Fault detection: A stuck cell is detected by monitoring the output of test vectors; its column is removed from the decomposition. The accuracy loss is proportional to the Frobenius norm of the removed column, which is negligible for typical redundancy designs.



